--TEST--
`Core\Request`'s refusal is an ordinary `Throwable` a program continues past, and reading twice refuses twice -- ADR 0012 § 7, `rule:errors/on-limit`, spec § 15
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The edge on the other side of the refusal: what a program can do *after*
// one. ADR 0012 § 7 asks for a throw rather than an empty value, and a throw
// is a rung of `rule:errors/escalation-ladder`'s ladder every `catch` sees -- so the program is still
// running here, which is what separates this from a fatal.
uint $steps = 0;
try {
    Core\Request::method();
} catch (LogicError $stopped) {
    $steps = $steps + 1;
}
$steps = $steps + 1;
echo "the program took ", $steps, " steps past the throw\n";

// Twice, because the absence of a request is a property of this program and
// not a state a read moves through: a refusal that consumed something would
// answer differently the second time, and a member that cached its own first
// answer would stop refusing.
uint $caught = 0;
try {
    Core\Request::path();
} catch (LogicError $first) {
    $caught = $caught + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::path();
} catch (LogicError $second) {
    $caught = $caught + 1;
}
echo "the same read refused ", $caught, " times\n";

// And a `catch` on one member does not stand in front of another: the throw
// carries no state that a later read of a different member could find already
// spent.
uint $after = 0;
try {
    Core\Request::query("page");
} catch (LogicError $third) {
    $after = $after + 1;
}
echo "a later read still refused ", $after, " time\n";

// Four throws in, the verb's second reader is still refusing: `isHead` reads
// the same token `method` did at the top of this program, and a member that
// answered `false` here would be reporting that the peer sent something other
// than `HEAD` -- which is a fact about a request there is none of.
uint $verb = 0;
try {
    Core\Request::isHead();
} catch (LogicError $fourth) {
    $verb = $verb + 1;
}
echo "the verb's second reader still refused ", $verb, " time\n";

// And the one reader whose successful path really does consume something:
// `body()` pulls the request's bytes to their end, so it is the member a
// half-spent or cached state would surface in first -- a second read that
// answered "" where the first threw would be reporting a body that arrived
// empty. There is no request here to spend, so both reads refuse alike.
uint $raw = 0;
try {
    Core\Request::body();
} catch (LogicError $fifth) {
    $raw = $raw + 1;
}
try {
    Core\Request::body();
} catch (LogicError $sixth) {
    $raw = $raw + 1;
}
echo "the body reader refused ", $raw, " times\n";
--EXPECT--
the program took 2 steps past the throw
the same read refused 2 times
a later read still refused 1 time
the verb's second reader still refused 1 time
the body reader refused 2 times
